-- Lesson 1: The Coordinate Revolution
'{"hook":"One morning, lying in bed watching a fly on the ceiling, a French philosopher realized he could describe the fly''s exact position with just two numbers. That insight merged algebra and geometry forever.","inventor":{"name":"René Descartes","birth_year":1596,"death_year":1650,"nationality":"French","bio":"French philosopher, mathematician, and scientist who published La Géométrie in 1637 as an appendix to his Discourse on the Method. He introduced the Cartesian coordinate system, allowing geometric shapes to be described with algebraic equations."},"year_invented":1637,"etymology":{"word":"Cartesian","from":"Latin","parts":[{"root":"Cartesius","meaning":"the Latinized form of Descartes''s name"}]},"story_paragraphs":["The famous story — possibly a legend — is that Descartes was lying in bed one morning watching a fly crawl across his ceiling. He realized that he could describe the fly''s exact position using two numbers: how far along the ceiling and how far across. This simple idea would revolutionize mathematics.","Before Descartes, algebra and geometry were separate subjects. Algebra dealt with equations and symbols; geometry dealt with shapes and drawings. No one had figured out how to connect them. Descartes''s coordinate system was the bridge.","In 1637, Descartes published La Géométrie, showing that every point on a flat surface could be described by two numbers (x, y), and every algebraic equation could be drawn as a geometric shape. The line y = 2x + 1 IS a picture. The circle x² + y² = 25 IS an equation.","Today, the Cartesian plane is so fundamental we barely notice it. Every graph, chart, map, GPS coordinate, video game, and touchscreen uses Descartes''s idea. When you tap a point on your iPad, its position is described by two coordinates — exactly as Descartes imagined."],"key_contributors":[{"name":"Pierre de Fermat","contribution":"Independently developed coordinate geometry around the same time as Descartes"},{"name":"Nicole Oresme","contribution":"Used coordinate-like graphs to plot velocity vs time in the 14th century, 300 years before Descartes"}],"real_world_applications":[{"name":"GPS Navigation","icon":"📍","description":"Every location on Earth is described by two coordinates: latitude and longitude"},{"name":"Video Games","icon":"🎮","description":"Every character and object in a game has an (x, y) position on screen"},{"name":"Touchscreens","icon":"📱","description":"When you tap your screen, the device records the exact (x, y) coordinate of your finger"},{"name":"Data Visualization","icon":"📊","description":"Bar charts, line graphs, scatter plots — all built on Descartes''s coordinate system"},{"name":"Architecture","icon":"🏗️","description":"Building plans use coordinate grids to specify exact positions of walls, doors, and windows"}],"image":{"url":null,"alt":"Portrait of René Descartes","attribution":"Wikimedia Commons, public domain","source_url":null}}'::jsonb)
